{
  "abstract": "For every odd integer $n\\ge5$ and every fixed smooth cyclic $n$-isogeny, we prove the rational Hodge conjecture for all powers of all but finitely many admissible members of an explicit elliptic-surface family. These surfaces have geometric genus $(n-1)/2$, giving families in every geometric genus at least two. They arise from the curve-square construction of Ingalls–Logan–Patashnick with a particular double-cover line bundle. The dominating genus-$2n+1$ curve has Jacobian isogenous to three elliptic factors and squares of two hyperelliptic Jacobians. The latter carry the product of the real cyclotomic fields indexed by nontrivial divisors of $n$. A Tate degeneration, direct boundary-monodromy calculation, and transport through the marked-isogeny parameter space give full connected monodromy on every factor. A maximal real-multiplication argument handles non-CM specializations, including quaternionic ones. André–Oort makes the higher-dimensional CM parameter sets finite. Ribet's and Hazama's results and the actual domination then prove algebraicity on all surface powers and their finite mixed products. For number-field inputs, a Hilbert subset also has the generic endomorphism algebras. We certify one specified degree-eleven member using exact Frobenius polynomials at 7 and 13, without assuming absolute simplicity. The surfaces have fibers $(n-1)I_4+(n+1)I_2+I_2^*$ and Picard number at least $4n+6$. Explicit equations and reproducible CPU checks are included. The finite exceptional set is not made effective, and the theorem neither covers every CM specialization nor resolves the general Hodge conjecture.",
